restart
------------------
Used to restart a container automatically under specified conditions.

services:
  backend:
    image: my-backend
    restart: unless-stopped

Common options include:
no            Don't automatically restart the container. Container starts -> Application crashes -> Container stops -> ❌ Docker does NOT restart it   This is the default behavior.
always        Use this when you want a service to continuously stay running.
on-failure    Restart only when the container exits with a failure/error.
unless-stopped

restart: always
Docker restarts the container if it crashes/fails OR stops.

1. Application crashes
Container running
      ↓
Application crashes
      ↓
Container stops
      ↓
Docker restarts it ✅
2. Container stops normally
Container running
      ↓
Container stops
      ↓
Docker restarts it ✅
3. You manually stop it     
docker stop mycontainer     
the container stays stopped (while docker is running(It means as long as the Docker Engine/daemon is running on your server.)
   But if the server restarts:
Server reboot
    ↓
Docker Engine starts
    ↓
Container starts again ✅
But if the Docker daemon restarts (for example, the server reboots), Docker can start that container again.
EC2 server
   ↓
RESTART / REBOOT
   ↓
Server comes back
   ↓
Docker starts
   ↓
Container with restart: always
   ↓
Starts again automatically ✅
So server restarts = the whole machine is rebooted, not just the Docker container.
Example:sudo reboot This reboots the Linux server.
Even if the container was stopped manually, Docker's restart behavior can cause it to be started again when the Docker daemon/Compose-managed setup is brought back.
Use this when you want a service to continuously stay running.

3. on-failure
restart: on-failure
Restart only when the container exits with a failure/error.

Example:
Application crashes
      ↓
Exit code = 1
      ↓
Docker restarts

But: Application stops normally
      ↓
Exit code = 0
      ↓
❌ Docker doesn't restart

You can also specify a maximum number of restart attempts:
restart: on-failure:5
Meaning: Try to restart it up to 5 times after failures.

Crash
The application unexpectedly stops because of an error.

Example:

Backend running
      ↓
Unexpected error
      ↓
Application crashes
      ↓
Container stops

Usually the container exits with a non-zero exit code, such as 1.

Failure

Failure is a broader term. It means the container/application exits unsuccessfully.

For example:

Application crashes → failure
Command returns error → failure
Application exits with exit code 1 → failure

When we say “application exits unsuccessfully”, it simply means:

The application stops and returns an exit code other than 0.

Application crashes
        ↓
Exit code 1
        ↓
✅ Restart


Application encounters an error
        ↓
Exit code 1
        ↓
✅ Restart


Application fails
        ↓
Non-zero exit code
        ↓
✅ Restart


Application finishes normally
        ↓
Exit code 0
        ↓
❌ Don't restart

Non-zero exit code means the application ended with an error/failure.
Docker uses exit codes:

0       → Success ✅
1, 2, 3... → Failure/Error ❌

A program can exit with different non-zero codes:
0   → Success
1   → General failure
2   → Another type of failure
127 → Command not found
130 → Terminated by Ctrl+C

4. unless-stopped
restart: unless-stopped

This means: Keep restarting the container unless I intentionally stop it.
Example:
Container crashes
      ↓
Docker restarts it

But if you intentionally do:
docker stop mycontainer
then Docker won't keep automatically restarting that container.

If restart: unless-stopped
If you manually stop it: docker stop mycontainer
then even after:
Server reboot
    ↓
Docker starts
    ↓
Container stays stopped ⏹️


Easy comparison
Policy  	Container crashes	Normal exit	Manually stopped
no	           ❌ No restart	❌              	❌
always	              ✅ Restart	✅ Restart	Special behavior on daemon restart
on-failure	       ✅ Restart	❌               	❌
unless-stopped      	✅ Restart	✅ Restart         	❌ Stay stopped

Normal exit means the application finishes its work and stops without an error.

Policy          Crash	                     Manual stop	         Server reboot
always   	✅ Restart	            ⏹️ Stays stopped now	✅ Starts again
unless-stopped	✅ Restart	                ⏹️ Stays stopped	⏹️ Stays stopped
on-failure	✅ Restart if non-zero  	⏹️ Stays stopped	Depends on its exit/restart state
no	         ❌	                       ⏹️	                          ⏹️

So if you want:

Crash → restart, but manually stopped → remain stopped even after server reboot
