database container









volumes:
  dbdata:

networks:
  frontend-network:
  backend-network:


If they don't already exist
You do not need to manually create them.
Docker Compose will automatically create them when you run:
docker compose up -d


If they already exist
This is different.
If you want Compose to use an already-existing Docker volume or network, you should tell Compose that it is external.

For example, an existing network:

networks:
  mynetwork:
    external: true

Then Docker Compose will not create it. It expects mynetwork to already exist.

Similarly, for an existing volume:
volumes:
  mydata:
    external: true


Why do we write them at the bottom?
Because here we are declaring the Docker resources that Compose should create/manage:

volumes:
  appdata:
  dbdata:

→ Create/manage these 2 volumes

networks:
  frontend-network:
  backend-network:



  backend-network:
    external: false
then Compose is allowed to create them if they don't already exist.

In fact, external: false is the default, so you normally don't need to write it.
false → Compose can create it
true → Compose expects it to already exist.

What if I write mynetwork under the service, but don't declare it at the bottom?
Then Compose will give an error.
but Compose doesn't know what mynetwork is.
You normally need to declare it at the bottom:

Compose checks mynetwork:
Doesn't exist? → ✅ Compose creates it.
Already exists as a Compose-managed network? → ✅ Compose uses it.
An independently created Docker network with the same name? → You generally need external: true if you want Compose to use that existing external network.

So simply:
networks:
  mynetwork:

1. No external
networks:
  mynetwork:

Compose manages this network.

Compose
   ↓
mynetwork
   ↓
Creates/manages it

If you run:

docker compose down

Compose can remove this network because it owns it.

2. external: true
networks:
  mynetwork:
    external: true

The network was created outside Compose:

docker network create mynetwork

Then Compose says:

"This already exists.
I will use it.
I will NOT manage/create it."

If you run:

docker compose down

Compose does not remove the external network.

If you write:

networks:
  mynetwork:

without external: true, Compose will create a Compose-managed network if the required Compose network doesn't exist.
Even if you already created a standalone Docker network called mynetwork, Compose does not automatically take ownership of that existing standalone network.

Example
You already created:
docker network create mynetwork

Then Compose has:
networks:
  mynetwork:
When you run:
docker compose up -d
Compose normally creates its own project-scoped network, such as:
myproject_mynetwork
The existing:
mynetwork
remains separate.

If you want to use the existing one
networks:
  mynetwork:
    external: true

Then Compose uses:

existing mynetwork
        ↓
     container

So:

external: true → use the existing external network
no external: true → Compose manages its own network

No external: true → Compose creates/manages/deletes it
external: true → Compose only uses it; doesn't create/delete it


So:

docker compose down
       ↓
Containers       ❌ deleted
Networks         ❌ deleted
Volumes          ✅ remain
Images           ✅ remain
Source code      ✅ remain
If you use -v
docker compose down -v

Then:

Containers       ❌ deleted
Networks         ❌ deleted
Volumes          ❌ deleted
Images           ✅ remain
Source code      ✅ remain

Easy to remember:

down → containers + Compose networks

down -v → containers + networks + volumes

Images are not deleted by down.

Example:

volumes:
  dbdata:

Compose creates dbdata.

Then:

docker compose down -v

➡️ dbdata is deleted.

But external volume is different
volumes:
  dbdata:
    external: true

Then:

docker compose down -v

➡️ dbdata is NOT deleted, because Compose does not manage that external volume.

So:

Compose-managed volume
        ↓
docker compose down -v
        ↓
❌ Deleted




External volume
        ↓
docker compose down -v
        ↓
✅ Remains



